<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đurađ Branković</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1377">1377</a>–<a href="/years/1456">1456</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as George Branković</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Đurađ Branković was Despot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1427 to 1456. He built the great fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> as his capital on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, balancing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The Ottomans overran Serbia in 1439, but he recovered it in 1444. Serbia fell to the Ottomans three years after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durad-brankovic.jpg" alt="Đurađ Branković" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%90ura%C4%91_Brankovi%C4%87,_Esphigmenou_charter_(1429).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đurađ Vuković Branković, also George Branković, was born on 22 February 1377, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> and Mara Lazarević, a daughter of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a>. His wife was Eirene Kantakouzene, a Byzantine princess. Despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> had appropriated lands that were part of Vuk's hereditary holdings, which led to a conflict between the two cousins that lasted ten years. After they reconciled, the childless Stefan made Đurađ his heir. When Stefan died on 19 July 1427 and Đurađ succeeded him, he was 50 and a man of great experience.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Stefan's death King Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, which was ceded to him, and Đurađ kept southern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macva">Mačva</a> with the king's approval. The Ottoman sultan answered the change of throne by sending an army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which took Niš and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevac">Kruševac</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a>. In 1429 John VIII Palaiologos formally granted Đurađ the title of Despot. Like other Christian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> he lived under Ottoman vassalage, but he often sought Christian alliances while keeping up the appearance of loyalty to the sultan.</p><p>In 1439 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, where he held large estates, and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>. Dubrovnik refused an Ottoman demand to hand him over, saying it was a free city that took in anyone who sought shelter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, who was married to Đurađ's daughter Mara, made peace with Hungary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a> in 1444. The peace restored Đurađ's rule, though he had to give his vast estates to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, and on 22 August 1444 he peacefully took possession of the evacuated Smederevo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The peace was broken by Hunyadi and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland">Władysław III of Poland</a> in the Crusade of Varna, and in 1448 Hunyadi's army was beaten by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> at Kosovo Polje. Because of a personal feud, Đurađ held Hunyadi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> for a short time on his retreat. After Hunyadi's victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 14 July 1456 Đurađ regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, but he died before the end of that year, aged 79. His library held Serbian, Slavonic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek manuscripts and made Smederevo a centre of Serbian culture. Following disputes among his widow and three sons, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed Serbia three years after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đurađ Branković” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was then briefly held captive by the Serbian despot <b>Đurađ Branković</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-20">20 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His sons, including <b>Đurađ Branković</b>, held part of his lands as Ottoman vassals and in 1412 made peace with Stefan Lazarević; the district merged into the Serbian Despotate, which Đurađ inherited in 1427.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-brankovic">District of Branković</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1427 the despotate passed to his nephew <b>Đurađ Branković</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-despotate">Serbian Despotate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. Died in Smederevo Fortress, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.67,21.17&amp;year=1428" aria-label="Pristina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Đurađ Branković"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4447 3904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4447 3904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.67,21.17&amp;year=1428">What was here in 1428? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
